"84_object_stream_completion.mst — the last census group: Object's copying
 and type predicates, the stream gaps, and one real bug found on the way.

 **The bug.** `#sym , 'x'` raised 'symbols are immutable', and so did
 `#sym copyFrom: 1 to: 2`. `String>>,` builds its result with
 `self class new:` — which for a Symbol receiver asks for a *Symbol*, and
 Symbols are interned and immutable. The classic Smalltalk answer is
 `species`: the class a COPY of me should be, which for Symbol is String.
 Adding it (and routing the copy-producing methods through it) fixes the
 whole family at once rather than special-casing `,`.

 **deepCopy handles cycles.** A naive recursive copy of a graph that points
 back at itself never terminates, and object graphs in a live image point
 back at themselves constantly. The map from original to copy is populated
 BEFORE the recursion descends, which is what makes a self-referencing
 structure copy in finite time — the ordering is the whole trick, so it is
 stated here rather than left to be re-derived.

 What deepCopy deliberately does NOT copy: SmallIntegers, Characters,
 Symbols, nil/true/false and classes. Copying a Symbol would break
 interning (`#a == #a` is relied on everywhere); copying a class would be
 nonsense. These are answered as themselves, which is also what makes the
 common case cheap.

 **storeOn:** answers an evaluable representation where one exists and says
 so plainly where it does not — a storeString that silently lies is worse
 than one that admits it cannot."

"Re-opening a class REQUIRES naming its true superclass — declaring
 `Object subclass: SequenceableCollection` does not extend it, it
 RE-PARENTS it, and every method it inherited from Collection vanishes.
 That mistake here broke detect:ifNone:, notEmpty and includes: across the
 image at once. Each superclass below was read off the original
 declaration, not assumed."
nil subclass: Object [
    "The class a COPY of me should be. Differs from `class` only where a
     copy cannot be the same species as the original — Symbol being the
     case that motivated it."
    species ^ <Object> [ ^self class ]

    "The write half of instVarAt:, which shipped read-only (prim 25). Without
     it there was no way to set a named instance variable, which is why a
     generic deepCopy could not copy a plain object's fields at all — the
     gap that stopped this file's first version. Prim 265 stores through the
     barriered path, so writing a young oop into a promoted object cannot
     leave a dangling reference."
    instVarAt: i <Integer> put: v <Object> ^ <Object> [
        <primitive: 265>
    ]

    "── deep copying ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    deepCopy ^ <Object> [
        "A copy whose reachable structure is copied too. Cycle-safe."
        ^self deepCopyWith: IdentityDictionary new
    ]

    "THE single entry, and deliberately not overridable: it owns the two
     invariants (atoms are their own copies; a graph is visited once) and
     then delegates the class-specific part. An earlier version let
     subclasses override THIS, and ArrayedCollection's override promptly
     skipped the atom check — which matters because Symbol IS an
     ArrayedCollection (String < ArrayedCollection, Symbol < String), so
     `(Array with: #s) deepCopy` tried to at:put: into a Symbol. Putting the
     guard where it cannot be bypassed is the fix; adding it to each
     override would have been the patch."
    deepCopyWith: seen <IdentityDictionary> ^ <Object> [
        self isDeepCopyAtom ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        (seen includesKey: self) ifTrue: [ ^seen at: self ].
        ^self deepCopyStructureWith: seen
    ]

    "The overridable half. Implementors MUST register their copy in `seen`
     before descending, or a self-referencing structure recurses forever."
    deepCopyStructureWith: seen <IdentityDictionary> ^ <Object> [
        | copy |
        copy := self shallowCopy.
        seen at: self put: copy.
        1 to: self class instVarNames size do: [ :i |
            copy instVarAt: i put: ((self instVarAt: i) deepCopyWith: seen) ].
        ^copy
    ]

    isDeepCopyAtom ^ <Boolean> [
        ^self isSymbol or: [ self isInteger or: [ self isCharacter
            or: [ self isNil or: [ self isBoolean or: [ self isClass ] ] ] ] ]
    ]

    "── store: an evaluable representation, honest about its limits ──────"

    storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [
        "The default cannot produce something evaluable, and says so rather
         than emitting a printString that looks like source and is not."
        aStream nextPutAll: '(', self class name asString, ' new "not storable")'.
        ^self
    ]
    storeString ^ <String> [
        | s |
        s := WriteStream on: String new.
        self storeOn: s.
        ^s contents
    ]

    "── the type predicates the set was missing ──────────────────────────
     isString/isSymbol/isInteger/isCharacter/isPoint already existed; these
     complete the set so a caller never has to guess which spellings this
     image happens to have."
    isCollection ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
    isNumber ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
    isBoolean ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
    isBlock ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
    isArray ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
    isDictionary ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
    isClass ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
]

Object subclass: Boolean [ isBoolean ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ] ]
Magnitude subclass: Number  [ isNumber  ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ] ]
Object subclass: BlockClosure [ isBlock ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ] ]
Object subclass: Behavior [
    isClass ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]
    "A class is its own species and its own deep copy — see isDeepCopyAtom."
    species ^ <Object> [ ^self ]
]

Object subclass: Collection [
    isCollection ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]
    storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [
        "Evaluable: `(OrderedCollection new add: 1; add: 2; yourself)`."
        aStream nextPutAll: '('; nextPutAll: self class name asString; nextPutAll: ' new'.
        self do: [ :e | aStream nextPutAll: ' add: '. e storeOn: aStream. aStream nextPut: $; ].
        aStream nextPutAll: ' yourself)'.
        ^self
    ]
    "Built FRESH rather than shallowCopy-then-overwrite, because
     `OrderedCollection>>shallowCopy` SHARES its internal storage (measured;
     Array's does not). Copying then writing each deep-copied element back
     through at:put: therefore wrote into the ORIGINAL too, and the two ended
     up pointing at the same copies — a deepCopy that silently was not one.
     Registering the fresh collection before filling it keeps cycles safe."
    deepCopyStructureWith: seen <IdentityDictionary> ^ <Object> [
        | copy |
        copy := self species new.
        seen at: self put: copy.
        self do: [ :e | copy add: (e deepCopyWith: seen) ].
        ^copy
    ]
]

"Symbol is the case that motivated `species`: a copy of a Symbol must be a
 String, because Symbols are interned and immutable. Without this,
 `#sym , 'x'` and `#sym copyFrom:to:` both raised 'symbols are immutable'
 — the copy-producing methods in String build their result with
 `self species new:`."
String subclass: Symbol [
    species ^ <Object> [ ^String ]
]

ArrayedCollection subclass: String [
    "Routed through species so a Symbol receiver produces a String. The two
     methods below are String's own copy producers; every other copier in
     the hierarchy already goes through one of them."
    , other <SequenceableCollection> ^ <Object> [
        | r |
        r := self species new: self size + other size.
        r replaceFrom: 1 to: self size with: self.
        r replaceFrom: self size + 1 to: self size + other size with: other.
        ^r
    ]
    copyFrom: from <Integer> to: to <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        | r n |
        n := to - from + 1.
        r := self species new: n.
        1 to: n do: [ :i | r at: i put: (self at: from + i - 1) ].
        ^r
    ]
    isString ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]
    storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [
        "A quoted literal, with embedded quotes doubled — evaluable."
        aStream nextPut: $'.
        1 to: self size do: [ :i |
            | c | c := self at: i.
            c = $' ifTrue: [ aStream nextPut: $' ].
            aStream nextPut: c ].
        aStream nextPut: $'.
        ^self
    ]
    "A String's elements are Characters, which are atoms — so copying the
     bytes IS the deep copy, with no element recursion to do."
    deepCopyStructureWith: seen <IdentityDictionary> ^ <Object> [
        | copy |
        copy := self shallowCopy.
        seen at: self put: copy.
        ^copy
    ]
]

"A fixed-size collection cannot be built by `add:`, so it takes the
 shallowCopy route — which is SAFE here precisely because an
 ArrayedCollection's shallowCopy does not share storage."
SequenceableCollection subclass: ArrayedCollection [
    deepCopyStructureWith: seen <IdentityDictionary> ^ <Object> [
        | copy |
        copy := self shallowCopy.
        seen at: self put: copy.
        1 to: self size do: [ :i |
            copy at: i put: ((self at: i) deepCopyWith: seen) ].
        ^copy
    ]
]

ArrayedCollection subclass: Array [
    isArray ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]
    storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [
        "Built forwards. An earlier version wrote the separator after every
         element and then backed up with `skip: -6` — but WriteStream has no
         skip:, and a stream you cannot rewind is the normal kind. Emitting
         the separator BEFORE each element except the first needs no
         rewinding at all."
        self isEmpty ifTrue: [ aStream nextPutAll: '(Array new: 0)'. ^self ].
        aStream nextPutAll: '(Array'.
        self do: [ :e |
            aStream nextPutAll: ' with: '.
            e storeOn: aStream ].
        aStream nextPut: $).
        ^self
    ]
]

HashedCollection subclass: Dictionary [
    isDictionary ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]
    "Keys are NOT deep-copied: a copied key would not be findable under the
     original key, which is the opposite of what a copy should mean. Values
     are. Fresh, for the same storage-sharing reason as Collection."
    deepCopyStructureWith: seen <IdentityDictionary> ^ <Object> [
        | copy |
        copy := self species new.
        seen at: self put: copy.
        self keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v | copy at: k put: (v deepCopyWith: seen) ].
        ^copy
    ]
    storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [
        aStream nextPutAll: '(Dictionary new'.
        self keysAndValuesDo: [ :k :v |
            aStream nextPutAll: ' at: '. k storeOn: aStream.
            aStream nextPutAll: ' put: '. v storeOn: aStream.
            aStream nextPut: $; ].
        aStream nextPutAll: ' yourself)'.
        ^self
    ]
]

"Literals store as themselves — the case where printString IS evaluable."
Magnitude subclass: Number [ storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [ aStream nextPutAll: self printString. ^self ] ]
Object subclass: Boolean [ storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [ aStream nextPutAll: self printString. ^self ] ]
Object subclass: UndefinedObject [ storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [ aStream nextPutAll: 'nil'. ^self ] ]
Magnitude subclass: Character [ storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [ aStream nextPutAll: self printString. ^self ] ]
String subclass: Symbol [ storeOn: aStream <WriteStream> ^ <Self> [ aStream nextPutAll: self printString. ^self ] ]

"── the stream gaps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

Object subclass: ReadStream [
    contents ^ <Object> [
        "The WHOLE underlying collection, not the unread remainder —
         Smalltalk's long-standing meaning for `contents` on a read stream,
         and the reason `upToEnd` exists for the other question."
        ^collection copyFrom: 1 to: collection size
    ]
    skipTo: anObject <Object> ^ <Boolean> [
        "Advance past the next occurrence; answer whether one was found. On
         failure the stream is left AT THE END, which is what makes a
         `[ stream skipTo: x ] whileTrue:` loop terminate."
        [ self atEnd ] whileFalse: [
            self next = anObject ifTrue: [ ^true ] ].
        ^false
    ]
    collect: aBlock <[Object,Object]> ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        "The remaining elements, transformed — consumes the stream."
        | out |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        [ self atEnd ] whileFalse: [ out add: (aBlock value: self next) ].
        ^out
    ]
]

Object subclass: WriteStream [
    position ^ <Integer> [ ^position ]
    reset ^ <Self> [
        "Rewind to the start; what has been written is forgotten rather than
         truncated, since the next write overwrites it."
        position := 0.
        ^self
    ]
]
